    impact: { magnitude: 4, latency: "weeks", durability: "lasting", effort: "high" },
    feeds: ["physical capability", "risk literacy", "achievement"],
    description:
      "Cohort studies on Kilimanjaro and Mount Whitney identify the determinants of summit success and acute mountain sickness (AMS), with ascent profile prominent; a meta-analysis found a previous history of AMS is a poor predictor of getting it again (summary sensitivity 0.50, specificity 0.72), so a prior good trip is no guarantee. Meta-analyses and a Cochrane review agree acetazolamide reduces AMS risk (Cochrane RR 0.47 versus placebo, with more paraesthesia), that 250 mg a day is the lowest dose with evidence (BMJ meta-analysis: NNT 6), and that benefit is largest when the baseline risk — fast ascent — is high. Ibuprofen has a smaller supporting literature. The honest boundary: prophylaxis is a clinician's decision; the slow ascent is yours.",
    action: "For any trek above 2,500 m, choose the itinerary with the most acclimatisation days (and a rest day every third day above 3,000 m), track symptoms with a written daily score, and book a pre-travel consultation to discuss prophylaxis at least a month out.",
