    description:
      "Evidence-based reviews set out the practical challenges of international travel with a chronic illness, and diabetes research shows that crossing time zones on insulin needs a specific plan (a pilot randomised trial compared two basal insulins for exactly this). For pregnancy, a systematic review and several cohort studies find no clear excess of adverse pregnancy outcomes from air travel in healthy women, but venous thromboembolism risk is raised by both pregnancy and long-haul flight, and a review estimates the baseline long-haul VTE risk at about 2.8 per 1,000 travellers before pregnancy adds to it. None of this is advice: it is the literature to bring to the appointment.",
    action: "If you have a chronic condition or are pregnant, book an appointment with your treating specialist at least a month before travel and leave with a written plan covering medication timing across time zones, what to do if unwell, insurance that names the condition, and — for long flights — the clot-prevention steps your clinician chooses.",
